// Test: error_statement/error_statement_traps_with_message
// Grammar: error_statement (foundation/spec/grammar.ebnf)
// Spec: foundation/spec/semantic-rules.md — SEM error()
//
// Regression guard for COMPILER-SYNTHETIC-MODULE-START-BLOCK-DROPPED
// (dashboard fingerprint 4e740d00a254, reported against 0.30.326+).
//
// Pre-fix the parser produced `Statement::Error { message }` but the HIR
// builder had no explicit handler — the variant fell through the
// wildcard `_ => Ok(HirStatement::Expression { Void })` arm and the
// entire statement (including the message argument) became a no-op
// void literal. Every `error("...")` call disappeared from the WASM,
// the message string vanished from the data section, and execution
// continued past the would-be trap.
//
// This was discovered as PAGE-COMPANION-NO-ROUTE-GENERATED's downstream
// guard never firing: frame.ui injects diagnostic synthetic modules
// containing `start:\n\terror("...")` to surface plugin-side build
// failures, but the entire start: body got swallowed by this bug.
//
// Fix lowers `error("msg")` to a `print(msg)` followed by a
// `require false` so:
//   - The message string is preserved in the data section.
//   - The host observes the message on stdout before the trap.
//   - `require false` always traps (the canonical Clean Language
//     way to express an unconditional runtime trap).
//
// This test is structural: the .cln file is just `start: error(...)`.
// Standalone execution exits 0 on the broken compiler (the trap is
// silently dropped) and non-zero on the fixed compiler (require false
// fails). A companion Rust test in tests/test_error_statement_traps.rs
// also asserts the WASM data section contains the message literal.

start:
	error("error-statement-must-trap")
